WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.370
AI homelab hardware is a set of tradeoffs across CPU, GPU, VRAM, system RAM, storage, networking, and power.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:16.788
The CPU still matters for general orchestration, data preparation, containers, indexing, and workloads that do not fit cleanly on the GPU.

00:00:16.788 --> 00:00:26.137
System RAM matters when models are loaded partially in memory, when multiple services run at once, or when you work with larger datasets.

00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:32.688
Storage matters because models, logs, containers, caches, backups, and experiments grow quickly.

00:00:32.688 --> 00:00:41.560
VRAM is often the constraint people notice first because it affects which models and context sizes can run efficiently on the GPU.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:48.521
More VRAM usually gives you more room for larger models, larger context windows, or multiple services.

00:00:48.521 --> 00:00:58.552
Quantized models can reduce memory requirements by representing model weights with fewer bits, often with tradeoffs in quality, speed, or behavior.

00:00:58.552 --> 00:01:06.127
Quantization can make local inference practical on modest hardware, but it is still a design choice, not magic.

00:01:06.127 --> 00:01:08.516
There is no single perfect machine.

00:01:08.516 --> 00:01:17.933
Integrated GPUs, consumer GPUs, used workstations, mini PCs, laptops, and external GPU setups can all be reasonable depending on the goal.

00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:22.983
A low-power system may be excellent for always-on services and embeddings.

00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:27.965
A gaming GPU may be useful for local language models and image workflows.

00:01:27.965 --> 00:01:33.015
A used workstation may offer expandability at the cost of power and noise.

00:01:33.015 --> 00:01:38.133
The right answer is the machine that fits the workload and the environment.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:40.999
Storage planning deserves early attention.

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:50.621
Separate space for models, datasets, generated outputs, container images, logs, backups, and experiment notes keeps the lab easier to manage.

00:01:50.621 --> 00:01:58.811
Fast local storage helps with model loading and data processing, while larger slower storage may be enough for archives.

00:01:58.811 --> 00:02:01.609
Networking should match the access model.

00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:06.317
A local-only lab has different needs than a multi-device LAN service.

00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:10.344
Good hardware planning is not about chasing specifications.

00:02:10.344 --> 00:02:15.052
It is about reducing friction for the work you actually intend to do.
